Stage 7: the same arms under BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8.

commit:   22b55b3f84b869b2e94d4a2fa3e9f5bffe83a281 (+ this slice)
why:      the cross-language comparison is re-taken on a controlled physical
          machine at the end of the POC, and that run should use the most
          reliable harness available rather than this slice's hand-rolled loop.
          This is that harness, covering every payload and every arm, plus the
          run that says whether the two agree here.

What BenchmarkDotNet does that ../Harness/Bench.cs does not:
  * it measures an empty method of the same shape and SUBTRACTS that overhead.
    The hand-rolled loop counts its own NoInlining dead-code guard, which
    inflates the memcpy floor rows most, those being the shortest;
  * warmup runs to a convergence criterion rather than a fixed 40 ms budget
    guessed to be long enough to reach tier 1;
  * it reports a 99.9% confidence interval and removes outliers, rather than
    min/median/max over seven rounds;
  * each benchmark runs in its own process;
  * MemoryDiagnoser reports Gen0/Gen1/Gen2 counts, which the hand-rolled
    harness does not collect at all.

What it does NOT do, and why the hand-rolled harness is kept beside it:
  per-benchmark process isolation is the opposite of README R4's interleaving.
  On this shared 4-vCPU container an isolated arm can land in a quiet minute
  while another lands in a busy one, which is the exact failure the round-robin
  loop exists to avoid. On a controlled machine with pinning and isolation that
  noise is gone and BenchmarkDotNet's isolation is the better choice. Here, the
  two harnesses agreeing is what makes either one trustworthy.

The arm table, the payload builders and the codec are REFERENCED from the
harness project, not re-emitted, so the two harnesses cannot drift in what
they measure (R1).

job:      BenchmarkDotNet defaults (no ShortRun), all 16 payloads,
          5 encode arms + 3 decode arms = 128 benchmarks.
baseline: gp-writeto for encode, gp-parse for decode. Ratio and RatioSD are
          BenchmarkDotNet's own, computed from the ratio distribution.

